{"availability":"available","full":"loctools/plurr","name":"plurr","description":"Cross-platform format specification + libraries for handling plurals/genders/conditionals for Go, Java, JS, Obj-C, Perl, PHP, Python, and Rust","category":"Other","language":"Rust","stars":81,"forks":11,"created":"2012-05-21T22:24:19Z","updated":"2021-02-01T03:04:36Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"","topics":["format","gender","go","golang","java","javascript","multi-language","named-placeholders","objective-c","perl","php","pluralization","plurals","plurr","python","rust"],"demo":"https://iafan.github.io/plurr-demo/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T16:06:23.280Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-08T16:06:23.280Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-08T16:06:23.280Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-08T16:06:23.280Z","stars":81,"forks":11}],"technologies":["Python","Rust","Go"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Handling plurals, genders and conditionals in strings is not a particularly favorite task for programmers for many reasons. There's no common agreement on how to handle such variants, so all existing approaches are either platform-specific (like gettext/po), or have limitations (for example you can't have multiple plurals in one string), or not particularly translator- friendly, like Java's MessageFormat/ChoiceFormat.","Plurr is a universal format specification for handling plurals, genders, conditionals and placeholders designed to be easy to support for developers and understandable for translators yet robust to support different language requirements. Plurr formatters are implemented in: Go, Java, JavaScript, Objective-C, Perl, PHP, Python, and Rust. Feel free to contribute to this project and provide support for your favorite languages."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/loctools/plurr/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"666063c05c75e6a3cdf65793c1b1997791f63d519b88886059f1bd2009f09df2","extractedAt":"2026-10-08T16:06: